<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">8605103</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2074354</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Transforming growth factor alpha and epidermal growth factor levels in bladder cancer and their relationship to epidermal growth factor receptor.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Mellon</surname><given-names>J. K.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Cook</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Chambers</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Neal</surname><given-names>D. E.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>Department of Urology, Freeman Hospital, Newcastle upon Tyne, UK.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>3</month><year>1996</year></pub-date><volume>73</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>654</fpage><lpage>658</lpage><abstract><p>We have examined levels of epidermal growth factor (EGF) and transforming growth factor alpha (TGF-alpha) in neoplastic and non-neoplastic bladder tissue using a standard radioimmunoassay technique. Tumour samples had much higher TGF-alpha levels compared with EGF and TGF-alpha levels in malignant tissue were significantly higher than in benign bladder samples. There was, in addition, a difference in mean EGF levels from 'normal' bladder samples from non-tumour bearing areas of bladder in patients with bladder cancer compared with 'normal' bladder tissue obtained at the time of organ retrieval surgery. Levels of EGF and TGF-alpha did not correlate with levels of EGF receptor (EGFR) as determined by a radioligand binding method but levels of TGF-alpha &#x0003e; 10 ng gm-1 of tumour tissue did correlate with EGFR positivity defined using immunohistochemistry. These data suggest that TGF-alpha is the likely ligand for EGFR in bladder tumours.</p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00033-0102.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="654" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00033-0103.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="655" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00033-0104.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="656" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00033-0105.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="657" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00033-0106.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="658" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>